      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2013 John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "Adipocytes, regulated by insulin, represent the major peripheral source of prolactin (PRL), which play a pivotal role in energy balance, acting on adipogenesis and lipolysis. The aim of this study was to investigate whether PRL was associated with obesity-related inflammatory status and metabolic parameters. The diagnostic and prognostic role of PRL for metabolic syndrome (MS) was assessed. The effects of short-term lifestyle therapy on PRL levels were evaluated."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SUBJECTS",
            "content": "Prolactin was assessed in 94 obese patients and compared with 40 healthy children (HS).Patients were followed up for 1 year. Receiver operating characteristics (ROC) analysis was employed to find the best cut-off values capable of identifying MS in obese children for PRL, IL-6 and TNF-α. Kaplan-Meier curves were also generated. Adjusted risk estimates for MS were calculated using Cox proportional hazard regression analysis. An obesity intervention programme was administered for 12 months."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Prolactin levels were lower in obese patients than controls (P < 0·0001). PRL was found to be inversely correlated with BMI, IL-6 and HOMA-IR, whereas a direct correlation was found with HDL values. At ROC analysis, PRL showed higher sensitivity and specificity than IL-6 and TNF-α in identifying MS in obese children. Cox proportional hazard regression analysis showed that PRL predicted MS independently of other potential confounders. The lifestyle intervention improved PRL and metabolic parameters."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Prolactin represents a prognostic marker for obese children and a predictive factor for progression to MS. PRL measurement may be useful as part of the endocrine work-up of obese children."
          }
        ]
      },
